WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.399
In this lesson, you'll learn how to create and run NAT workflows.

00:00:03.798 --> 00:00:05.198
You'll create and run a minimal workflow

00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:06.799
and you'll serve it as a REST API,

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.122
showing how NAT brings production capabilities

00:00:09.122 --> 00:00:11.599
to even the most simple of workflows.

00:00:11.852 --> 00:00:13.752
Let's dive in.

00:00:13.752 --> 00:00:15.902
The NAT CLI gives you the ability to

00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:18.051
run agents quickly and serve them as APIs

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:20.551
that can be consumed by other services.

00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:22.602
And in this lesson, we're going to

00:00:22.602 --> 00:00:24.352
build a simple but production-ready NAT agent,

00:00:24.352 --> 00:00:26.078
and we're going to use the NAT

00:00:26.078 --> 00:00:28.051
CLI to serve that agent as an API,

00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:29.459
and we're going to connect it

00:00:29.459 --> 00:00:30.751
to a UI and watch it run.

00:00:31.006 --> 00:00:33.805
At the heart of an NAT agent workflow

00:00:33.805 --> 00:00:35.606
is a configuration file.

00:00:35.606 --> 00:00:37.805
This is a simple YAML file

00:00:37.805 --> 00:00:40.506
that lets us quickly change how our agent runs.

00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:43.506
In the example on this slide, we have two properties,

00:00:43.506 --> 00:00:45.206
a workflow and LLMs.

00:00:45.206 --> 00:00:49.206
The LLMs define and configure how we connect to large language models.

00:00:49.206 --> 00:00:51.305
In this case, an NVIDIA NIM container.

00:00:51.305 --> 00:00:55.006
It could be something else like OpenAI or Gemini,

00:00:55.564 --> 00:00:59.363
and a workflow. The workflow defines what the agent does.

00:01:00.064 --> 00:01:02.070
In this case, it's a react_agent and

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.364
it uses the LLM that we defined below.

00:01:04.763 --> 00:01:07.063
The configurations can be simple or they can be complex.

00:01:07.364 --> 00:01:11.063
They can contain evaluation information, observability information,

00:01:11.462 --> 00:01:14.262
They can define multiple tools for the agent to call

00:01:14.561 --> 00:01:17.462
and many other features such as retrievers or rerankers.

00:01:17.762 --> 00:01:19.361
Or they can be simple like this one,

00:01:19.561 --> 00:01:21.162
a workflow and some LLMs.

00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:23.962
To run these workflows, we use the NAT CLI.

00:01:24.361 --> 00:01:27.561
And you can see listed here several of the CLI commands.

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:31.804
nat run will run the workflow with a simple input.

00:01:32.004 --> 00:01:34.504
When you're ready to serve it as an API server,

00:01:34.504 --> 00:01:37.203
you run nat serve against the same configuration file.

00:01:37.804 --> 00:01:41.203
If you want to run evaluations in CI/CD or just by yourself,

00:01:41.203 --> 00:01:43.203
you would run nat eval.

00:01:43.504 --> 00:01:45.230
And if you want to check and

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.203
optimize your workflow, you can run nat optimize.

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:49.602
And to check that your YAML file is valid,

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:52.001
you can run nat validate.

00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:56.001
In this notebook, we're going to create our first NAT workflow.

00:01:56.402 --> 00:01:59.302
If you're running this notebook on your own machine,

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:01.701
you'll want to run the contents of this cell.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.391
to install the NeMo Agent Toolkit and the LangChain dependency.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.175
In this environment, it's already set

00:02:08.175 --> 00:02:09.790
up for you and ready to go.

00:02:09.991 --> 00:02:12.491
First, we're going to create the NAT configuration.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.191
The configuration is a YAML file,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.514
and in this YAML file, we

00:02:17.514 --> 00:02:19.991
have two top-level properties: LLMs and workflow.

00:02:20.556 --> 00:02:24.756
Remember that NAT composes these together into your agentic workflow.

00:02:25.156 --> 00:02:26.355
And so the first thing we're

00:02:26.355 --> 00:02:27.756
going to look at are the LLMs.

00:02:28.056 --> 00:02:30.456
You could have multiple LLMs, and in our case, this

00:02:30.456 --> 00:02:32.855
is a simple agentic workflow, and we just have one.

00:02:33.355 --> 00:02:35.056
And I've called it climate_llm.

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:37.921
Each LLM has to have a type.

00:02:38.222 --> 00:02:40.206
This tells the NeMo Agent Toolkit

00:02:40.206 --> 00:02:42.521
how to connect to the LLM provider.

00:02:42.722 --> 00:02:44.622
It could be OpenAI, it could be

00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:46.521
Google or any number of other providers.

00:02:46.622 --> 00:02:50.722
In our case, we've chosen nim, which is an NVIDIA inference container.

00:02:51.222 --> 00:02:54.722
And the type will determine what properties you give the LLM.

00:02:54.921 --> 00:02:57.521
For instance, it might need an OpenAI API key.

00:02:57.605 --> 00:02:59.605
In our case, we need a model_name,

00:03:00.105 --> 00:03:02.406
which is meta/llama-3.1-70b-instruct.

00:03:02.806 --> 00:03:05.406
We'll give it a base_url to tell it where to call,

00:03:05.705 --> 00:03:08.806
an api_key, and we've decided to set temperature

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.760
to 0.7 and max tokens to 2048.

00:03:13.661 --> 00:03:15.621
Now we have our LLM ready to

00:03:15.621 --> 00:03:17.860
go to be wired into our agentic workflow.

00:03:18.061 --> 00:03:22.183
So let's look at the workflow. Again we have a type defined.

00:03:22.484 --> 00:03:24.007
There are many built-in types and

00:03:24.007 --> 00:03:25.783
of course you can create your own.

00:03:25.883 --> 00:03:28.484
In this case, it's a built-in type called chat_completion.

00:03:28.683 --> 00:03:32.183
This is a simple input output response to the LLM.

00:03:32.787 --> 00:03:34.856
And chat_completion needs an LLM,

00:03:35.127 --> 00:03:37.366
so we will pass it the climate_llm

00:03:37.366 --> 00:03:39.926
that we defined earlier and a system prompt.

00:03:40.127 --> 00:03:42.956
You are a knowledgeable climate science assistant.

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:45.456
When we run this workflow, NeMo Agent Toolkit

00:03:45.456 --> 00:03:47.456
is going to look at this configuration file.

00:03:47.765 --> 00:03:50.566
It's going to load the chat_completion workflow type.

00:03:50.665 --> 00:03:52.566
It's going to create the LLM that we defined

00:03:52.566 --> 00:03:54.265
and pass it into that workflow type,

00:03:54.366 --> 00:03:56.665
and then it's going to run whatever input we have

00:03:56.665 --> 00:03:59.165
combined with the system_prompt and give us the output.

00:03:59.288 --> 00:04:03.088
Let's write this configuration file. Now we can run the workflow.

00:04:03.389 --> 00:04:04.939
There are many different ways as we'll

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:06.489
see over the course of this course

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:08.150
to run workflows. In this case,

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.088
we're going to use the NAT CLI

00:04:10.088 --> 00:04:12.389
and we're going to use the run command.

00:04:12.606 --> 00:04:14.907
This does a simple run against a workflow.

00:04:15.407 --> 00:04:17.022
We'll pass it the configuration file

00:04:17.022 --> 00:04:18.906
that we saved in the previous cell,

00:04:19.206 --> 00:04:20.906
and we will pass an input.

00:04:21.406 --> 00:04:24.206
So we can see how the NeMo Agent Toolkit has

00:04:24.406 --> 00:04:26.906
started to run from that configuration file.

00:04:27.379 --> 00:04:30.180
And we have some output about the configuration.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.295
Number of LLMs is one, which

00:04:32.295 --> 00:04:34.180
is the one we set up earlier.

00:04:34.779 --> 00:04:37.180
And we can see the workflow result.

00:04:38.379 --> 00:04:40.480
What is the difference between weather and climate?

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.910
and it's passed it to our

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.579
LLM and it's returned us an answer.

00:04:43.779 --> 00:04:46.579
So that's our first NeMo Agent Toolkit run.

00:04:46.903 --> 00:04:49.103
Let's go ahead and ask a second question.

00:04:50.302 --> 00:04:52.403
In this case, we'll use the same configuration file,

00:04:52.702 --> 00:04:54.549
but the input is changed to

00:04:54.549 --> 00:04:56.702
How much has global average temperature increased

00:04:56.702 --> 00:04:59.097
since pre-industrial times? and let's run

00:04:59.097 --> 00:05:01.891
this. And we can see the output.

00:05:02.791 --> 00:05:04.391
And let's run it one more time.

00:05:04.492 --> 00:05:08.192
This time, let's give it a specific question

00:05:08.192 --> 00:05:10.391
that would benefit from real data analysis.

00:05:10.869 --> 00:05:12.769
In this case, our LLM is going

00:05:12.769 --> 00:05:14.668
to be constrained by its training data,

00:05:14.769 --> 00:05:17.369
so I don't have high expectations for the results.

00:05:18.369 --> 00:05:20.769
As we can see, the LLM tried its best.

00:05:21.048 --> 00:05:24.348
But I don't believe that these numbers are correct.

00:05:24.848 --> 00:05:26.848
Notice a limitation here.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:28.949
Our agent is just an LLM call,

00:05:29.048 --> 00:05:31.149
it doesn't have any access to external data,

00:05:31.249 --> 00:05:34.348
just general knowledge whatever the LLM was trained with.

00:05:34.574 --> 00:05:36.855
We see that it cites some sources,

00:05:37.274 --> 00:05:39.175
but these sources are dated

00:05:39.555 --> 00:05:42.074
and it possibly could have hallucinated too.

00:05:42.774 --> 00:05:46.074
Now that we have this NeMo Agent Toolkit configuration,

00:05:46.475 --> 00:05:48.074
let's deploy it as an API.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.350
In production, you would run nat serve

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.949
in a terminal. However, this is running in a Jupyter notebook environment,

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:59.373
and so we are going to

00:05:59.373 --> 00:06:01.850
have to run it as a subprocess.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.100
We're dealing with subprocesses because this is a Jupyter notebook,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.000
but the command that we're running is nat serve

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.300
and we're passing it the configuration file.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:14.701
Previously we've run nat run and passed a single input.

00:06:14.937 --> 00:06:18.437
This serve is going to serve that agentic workflow

00:06:18.437 --> 00:06:20.437
as a self-contained API.

00:06:20.937 --> 00:06:22.819
And we see there our server's running in

00:06:22.819 --> 00:06:24.937
the background, so now we can test the API.

00:06:25.716 --> 00:06:27.910
In order to test the API, we're

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:30.416
going to use a standard Python requests library.

00:06:30.916 --> 00:06:34.016
And if you're familiar with the OpenAI API

00:06:34.016 --> 00:06:37.416
chat completions endpoints, then this will be familiar to you.

00:06:37.694 --> 00:06:40.595
Our server is running on localhost on port 8000

00:06:41.495 --> 00:06:46.194
and we're going to call /v1/chat/completions.

00:06:46.995 --> 00:06:49.894
And for our input, we will pass a messages

00:06:50.495 --> 00:06:53.194
and a single message and let's ask a climate message,

00:06:53.194 --> 00:06:56.694
what causes El Niño and how does it affect global weather.

00:06:57.177 --> 00:06:59.076
and we'll tell it not to stream back results

00:06:59.276 --> 00:07:01.276
so we can get it in a self-contained JSON.

00:07:01.976 --> 00:07:03.976
And once we get a 200 status code,

00:07:04.177 --> 00:07:07.677
then we can parse the OpenAI API compatible response,

00:07:08.408 --> 00:07:12.208
choices, the first choice, the message and then the content.

00:07:12.697 --> 00:07:15.497
And if there were an error, we could print out the error.

00:07:15.898 --> 00:07:17.898
Let's run this and see our API in

00:07:17.898 --> 00:07:19.898
action. And there we get our response back,

00:07:20.197 --> 00:07:23.298
describing El Niño being a complex weather phenomenon.

00:07:23.634 --> 00:07:25.283
So we've seen how to run the

00:07:25.283 --> 00:07:26.934
NeMo Agent Toolkit with a single input.

00:07:27.134 --> 00:07:29.134
We've seen how to serve it as an API,

00:07:29.434 --> 00:07:32.434
and now we'll see how to launch a UI

00:07:32.634 --> 00:07:35.434
that we can interact with our agentic workflow

00:07:35.634 --> 00:07:37.434
using a chat interface.

00:07:37.846 --> 00:07:41.446
The NeMo Agent Toolkit API can be used by any UI.

00:07:41.646 --> 00:07:46.346
However, there is a NeMo Agent Toolkit UI that is production ready.

00:07:46.745 --> 00:07:49.045
Showing that from a Jupyter notebook can be complicated,

00:07:49.045 --> 00:07:51.846
so we have the ui_manager class here that will help us

00:07:52.146 --> 00:07:53.946
show it from the Jupyter notebook.

00:07:54.389 --> 00:07:56.889
The NeMo Agent Toolkit ships with a rich UI,

00:07:57.189 --> 00:07:58.689
but we're going to look at that

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:00.189
more in depth in a later lesson.

00:08:00.588 --> 00:08:02.588
For now, let's just take a quick peek at the UI

00:08:02.588 --> 00:08:05.889
as it talks to the simple climate assistant that we created.

00:08:06.302 --> 00:08:09.103
This UI might be somewhat familiar to you

00:08:09.103 --> 00:08:10.903
if you've used other chatbot UIs.

00:08:11.403 --> 00:08:13.202
It allows you to create new chats,

00:08:13.202 --> 00:08:14.802
search through existing chats,

00:08:14.903 --> 00:08:16.603
connect to MCP servers,

00:08:16.603 --> 00:08:18.802
import data, export data, etc.

00:08:19.103 --> 00:08:21.103
And right now, it's connected to

00:08:21.103 --> 00:08:23.702
the simple climate assistant that we created.

00:08:24.557 --> 00:08:27.557
Let me send a prompt to our naive climate agent.

00:08:29.257 --> 00:08:31.858
What's the weather like in Florida?

00:08:32.557 --> 00:08:36.057
Since our agent isn't grounded in any real world data sets,

00:08:36.258 --> 00:08:38.857
it's just going to draw from the LLM's knowledge,

00:08:38.857 --> 00:08:42.158
and we see that it gives some general information about Florida weather.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:44.230
You'll notice that it gives me

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.061
a follow-up so I can continue chatting.

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.911
And even though our agent is simple, you can see that

00:08:48.911 --> 00:08:51.461
adding a UI on top of it gives you powerful tools

00:08:51.461 --> 00:08:53.260
to interact with an agentic workflow.

00:08:53.561 --> 00:08:55.010
Over the series of notebooks that we're

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:56.461
going to work on in this course,

00:08:56.461 --> 00:08:59.461
we're going to build a very powerful climate and mathematical agent

00:08:59.561 --> 00:09:03.160
that can answer deep questions about climate data over several years.

00:09:03.461 --> 00:09:04.961
And with this UI, we'll be able

00:09:04.961 --> 00:09:06.461
to chat in depth about that data.

00:09:07.398 --> 00:09:09.498
All right, let's stop our server

00:09:09.498 --> 00:09:11.398
and clean things up for the next notebook.

00:09:11.998 --> 00:09:15.998
In this notebook, we have built a simple but working climate assistant

00:09:15.998 --> 00:09:19.398
that is able to answer general questions about climate data.

00:09:19.679 --> 00:09:21.457
We served it as an API and

00:09:21.457 --> 00:09:23.490
we even consumed it in a chatbot UI.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:25.759
It's limited, it doesn't have access

00:09:25.759 --> 00:09:27.590
to real data, but it's a beginning.

00:09:27.889 --> 00:09:30.690
Next, we're going to add tools for actual climate data analysis.
